#{[17592186504607 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504607 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342648" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504607 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504607 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $g(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x-b}-\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b}}, & x\\ne 0, \\\\ 10, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor what value of $b$ is the function continuous at $x=0?$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504607 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1326dade-f2c7-5d3e-a020-cedd74fc345f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504607 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the limit, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x-b}-\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x-b}-\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b}}{\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b})}{(\\sqrt{x-b})^{2}-(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b})}{x-2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b})}{x(1-2x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{x}(\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b})}{\\cancel{x}(1-2x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b}}{1-2x} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{-b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x) & =g(0) \\\\ 2\\sqrt{-b} & =10 \\\\ \\sqrt{-b} & =5 \\\\ -b & =25 \\\\ b & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $b=-25$ removes the point discontinuity." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504611 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504611 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107916" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504611 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504611 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{cx+4}, & x\\ne -1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{27}, & x=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1,$ what is the value of $c?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504611 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "215b8d43-0fa8-5bc5-9a2a-4cbd17fc3abb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504611 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{cx+4}={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-c} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-c} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3} \\\\ 4-c & =3 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504617 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504617 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342333" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504617 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504617 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6k-20{x}^{2}, & x\\ne 1, \\\\ -8, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$ \n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504617 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "354ae055-47d1-5393-8d47-099f76806cac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504617 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 1}(6k-20{x}^{2})=6k-20.\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\ 6k-20 & =-8 \\\\ 6k & =12 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=2$ removes the point discontinuity." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504620 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504620 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504620 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504620 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $h(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(x-c)^{2}+1, & x\\ne 2, \\\\ 2, & x=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$, what are the possible values of $c?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504620 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3bf93bdf-40a6-507b-8e4d-2fa269000a75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504620 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $h(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}((x-c)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(2-c)^{2}+1 \\\\  & ={c}^{2}-4c+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}h(x) & =h(2) \\\\ {c}^{2}-4c+5 & =2 \\\\ {c}^{2}-4c+3 & =0 \\\\ (c-3)(c-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $c=3$ or $c=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504621 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504621 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48417" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504621 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504621 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+k}-\\sqrt{k}}, & x\\ne 0, \\\\ 18, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, what is the value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504621 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c7cc171-f78e-5279-bfdd-075dc2385743" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504621 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+k}-\\sqrt{k}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+k}-\\sqrt{k}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x+k}+\\sqrt{k}}{\\sqrt{x+k}+\\sqrt{k}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+k}+\\sqrt{k})}{x+k-k} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{x}(\\sqrt{x+k}+\\sqrt{k})}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\sqrt{x+k}+\\sqrt{k}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+k}+\\sqrt{k} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & =f(0) \\\\ 2\\sqrt{k} & =18 \\\\ \\sqrt{k} & =9 \\\\ k & =81.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504628 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504628 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48283" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504628 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504628 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+k}, & x\\ne 0, \\\\ 2, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0,$ what is the value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504628 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "550986e3-6ff5-514b-90ba-699e6397aa6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504628 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+k})=\\sqrt{k}.\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & =f(0) \\\\ \\sqrt{k} & =2 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504630 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504630 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342512" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504630 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504630 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $g(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{6}^{2x+a}, & x\\ne 0, \\\\ 36, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=0,$ what is the the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504630 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "571bd0c6-54b5-542b-a105-2bb5a6fcaaf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504630 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $g(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}{6}^{2x+a}={6}^{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x) & =g(0) \\\\ {6}^{a} & =36 \\\\ {6}^{a} & ={6}^{2} \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $a=2$ removes the point discontinuity." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504638 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504638 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107604" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504638 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504638 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3a-2ax, & x\\ne -2, \\\\ 14, & x=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2,$ what is the value of $a?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504638 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b9e1a36-03a5-51c4-becb-f3f4b938141f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504638 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}(3a-2ax) \\\\  & =3a-2a(-2) \\\\  & =7a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x) & =f(-2) \\\\ 7a & =14 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504647 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504647 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48544" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504647 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504647 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡3(x-k), & x\\ne 1, \\\\ 2, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ what is the value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504647 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "819d9389-7897-53fe-92be-f89ba9882964" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504647 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}{log}_{3}⁡3(x-k) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡3(1-k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡3(1-k) & =2 \\\\ 3(1-k) & ={3}^{2} \\\\ 1-k & =3 \\\\ k & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=-2$ removes the point discontinuity." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504651 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504651 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107915" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504651 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504651 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x-2}, & x\\ne 4, \\\\ c, & x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4,$ what is the value of $c?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504651 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "88e928a1-3941-5cdc-9fa6-49a8d4b468c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504651 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x-2}={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=4,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & =f(4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =c.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504677 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504677 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504677 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342456" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504677 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504677 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(a-x)^{2}-a, & x\\ne -3, \\\\ 9, & x=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3,$ **in ascending order**, what are the possible values of $a?$\n\n$a=$ \n{{field-1}}\nor\n$a=$ \n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504677 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4cdcaf7-2982-5947-b7f9-f0933f5cb1a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504677 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -3}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -3}((a-x)^{2}-a) \\\\  & =(a+3)^{2}-a \\\\  & ={a}^{2}+5a+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-3,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -3}f(x) & =f(-3) \\\\ {a}^{2}+5a+9 & =9 \\\\ {a}^{2}+5a & =0 \\\\ a(a+5) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $a=-5$ or $a=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504699 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504699 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48496" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504699 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504699 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(c-x)^{2}+3c, & x\\ne -1, \\\\ -5, & x=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$, what are the possible values of $c?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504699 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e241dd2f-cf9d-55b1-810e-b45db1f40880" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504699 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}((c-x)^{2}+3c) \\\\  & =(c-(-1))^{2}+3c \\\\  & ={c}^{2}+2c+1+3c \\\\  & ={c}^{2}+5c+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\ {c}^{2}+5c+1 & =-5 \\\\ {c}^{2}+5c+6 & =0 \\\\ (c+2)(c+3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $c=-2$ or $c=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
